
SylkServer WebRTC gateway
-------------------------

The WebRTC gateway application (webrtcgateway) lets WebRTC clients use SIP
accounts without a SIP stack of their own: they register, call SIP
endpoints, take part in video conferences and exchange messages, while the
gateway speaks SIP on their behalf. Clients talk to it with the sylkRTC
protocol over a WebSocket and with an HTTPS API; the media goes through
Janus.

SylkServer must run on a host with a publicly reachable IP address.

Ready to go clients: Sylk Mobile (Android, iOS), sylk-desktop and the Sylk
web client. Blink (macOS, Linux) uses the gateway for video conferencing,
the message journal and the account storage. See "Client libraries" below
for writing your own.


Architecture
------------

 +--------+  sylkRTC   +-----------+  Janus API  +--------------------+
 | WebRTC |  WebSocket |  Sylk     |  WebSocket  |  Janus             |
 | client +<---------->+  Server   +<----------->+  videoroom (SFU)   |
 |        |  HTTPS API |  webrtc   |             |  SIP plugin        |
 +---+----+            |  gateway  |             |  NoSIP plugin      |
     |                 |           |             +---------+----------+
     |  ICE/DTLS-SRTP  |           |                       |  RTP/SRTP
     +---------------->|  SIP:     |                       |
       (to Janus)      |  janus or |   SIP (own stack)     |   +----------+
                       |  sipsimple+---------------------------+  SIP     |
                       |  backend  |                       +-->+  A/V     |
                       |           |   SIP (Janus plugin)      |  client  |
                       |           |  .....................    +----------+
                       +-----+-----+                           +----------+
                             |   SIP MESSAGE, MSRP             |  SIP     |
                             +-------------------------------->+  Message |
                             |                                 |  client  |
                       +-----+---------------+   MSRP          +----------+
                       | Sylk conference     |<--------------> SIP chat
                       | (MSRP switch)       |                 clients
                       +---------------------+
                       +---------------------+   XMPP
                       | Sylk XMPP gateway   |<--------------> XMPP clients
                       +---------------------+

The media of a call goes from the browser (ICE, DTLS-SRTP) to Janus, and from
Janus as plain RTP or SRTP to the SIP endpoint. The gateway does no
transcoding.


SIP backends
------------

The SIP side of a WebRTC client's account (registration, calls) is done by
one of two backends, chosen per account:

 * sipsimple (the default): SylkServer's own SIP stack (SIP SIMPLE SDK)
   does the signalling, and Janus only the media, through the NoSIP
   plugin, which converts between the WebRTC PeerConnection and plain
   RTP/SRTP.
   sip_backends/sipsimple.py. Each account registers a contact pointing to
   this SylkServer (x-sylk-app=webrtcgateway), so requests to it reach the
   gateway. Implemented: outgoing calls with early media, incoming calls,
   re-INVITEs both ways, in-dialog MESSAGE, DTMF with SIP INFO, and MSRP
   chat offered next to audio/video (SylkServer is the MSRP endpoint).

 * janus: the Janus SIP plugin (Sofia-SIP) does both the SIP signalling and
   the media. Every registered account has a SIP plugin handle, every call
   a handle of its own. sip_backends/sofia.py.

Both backends implement the same interface (sip_backends/base.py), so the
clients see no difference. The backend is chosen with [General] sip_backend,
overridden for some accounts or domains with sip_backend_accounts
(alice@example.com:janus, test.example.com:janus), and, with
sip_backend_override enabled, by the client in account-add (sip_backend).


End-point requirements
----------------------

There is no transcoding: after the WebRTC specific media features (ICE,
DTLS) are taken care of, the endpoints exchange RTP directly, so SIP
endpoints must support Opus for audio and H.264, VP8 or VP9 for video (the
video codec of a room is set per room, see video_codec).

Any browser with WebRTC support works as a client, as do the native Sylk
clients.


Features
--------

Accounts and calls

 * SIP account registration (or none: an account can be added just to use
   the conferences and the messaging, without forking calls to the gateway)
 * One-to-one audio and video calls between WebRTC and SIP endpoints, early
   media, DTMF, re-INVITEs, in-call messages, MSRP chat (sipsimple backend)
 * Push notifications for incoming calls and messages (Firebase, Apple
   PushKit), through the Sylk push server (sylk_push_url); a push proxy
   under /webrtcgateway/push
 * Authentication with the SIP credentials, or against an IMAP server
   ([Auth])

Video conferencing

 * Rooms on the Janus videoroom plugin (an SFU): audio, video, screen
   sharing, chat and file sharing in the room
 * Several videos per participant (gateway feature multiple-video-feeds)
 * Moderation: speakers, mute one or all participants, raised hands,
   invite and remove participants
 * SIP participants through the conference audio bridge, with their audio
   levels
 * Per room policies, bitrate, codec, recording and participants invited
   when the room starts ([room:...] sections)

Messaging

 * One-to-one messages between WebRTC and SIP endpoints, with delivery and
   display notifications (IMDN) and composing indications
 * The message journal: every message and operation (removal, read,
   conversation removal, bulk delete) of an account, kept by the server so
   that all its devices, WebRTC and SIP, stay in sync and get what they
   missed while offline
 * File transfers over HTTPS: upload, download, cancel, expiry
   (filetransfer_expire_days)
 * Location sharing (application/sylk-location-sharing): the coordinates
   are end-to-end encrypted, the server reads only the envelope
 * End-to-end encryption with OpenPGP: public key lookup, and escrow of the
   private key for the account's own devices

Account data

 * The addressbook (XCAP, xcap_url), shared by all the Sylk and Blink
   clients, fetched and updated over the WebSocket
 * Account storage: a private per-account object store over HTTPS
   ([AccountStorage]): contact avatars (namespace avatars), the PGP private
   key (keys), the devices of the account (devices); changes are announced
   to the account's devices with application/sylk-storage-changed
 * The devices that use an account, when and from where (docs/ACCOUNT-DEVICES.md)

Management

 * An admin API and web interface (http_management_interface,
   https_management_interface): rooms and participants (kick, mute, audio
   levels, events), sessions, endpoints, push tokens, storage, file
   transfers, accounts (info, purge), daily metrics


SylkRTC API
-----------

WebSocket: wss://<host>/webrtcgateway/ws, subprotocol sylkRTC-2.

Requests are JSON objects with a sylkrtc name and a transaction id, answered
with an ack or an error. Everything else is an event: ready-event (with the
features of the gateway), account-event, session-event, videoroom-event.

  account     account-add, account-remove, account-register,
              account-unregister, account-devicetoken, account-message,
              account-disposition-notification, account-sync-conversations,
              account-mark-conversation-read, account-remove-message,
              account-remove-conversation, account-fetch-addressbook,
              account-update-addressbook, account-store-private-key,
              account-fetch-private-key, lookup-public-key
  calls       session-create, session-answer, session-trickle,
              session-terminate, session-message, session-dtmf-info,
              session-update
  rooms       videoroom-join, videoroom-leave, videoroom-configure,
              videoroom-feed-attach, videoroom-feed-answer,
              videoroom-feed-detach, videoroom-session-trickle,
              videoroom-session-update, videoroom-message,
              videoroom-composing-indication, videoroom-invite,
              videoroom-remove, videoroom-mute-participant,
              videoroom-mute-audio-participants, videoroom-toggle-hand

The definitions are in sylk/applications/webrtcgateway/models/sylkrtc.py.

HTTPS, below /webrtcgateway, authorised with the account's API token:

  /messages/history/<account>        the message journal (since a message id)
  /messages/history/<account>/remove remove entries from it
  /messages/summary/<account>        the journal counted by content type
  /transfers/summary/<account>       the files kept for the account
  /filetransfer/...                  file transfers (upload, download, cancel)
  /storage/<account>/...             the account storage
  /push/...                          the push proxy

The API token is handed out over SIP: a SIP device sends, through the
trusted SIP Proxy, a SIP MESSAGE of type application/sylk-api-token to
SylkServer, and gets back the token and the URLs. Every request with the
token may say which device sends it (X-Sylk-Device-Id, X-Sylk-Device-Name).


Client libraries
----------------

  JavaScript (desktop, web)  https://github.com/AGProjects/sylkrtc.js
                             used by sylk-desktop and the Sylk web client
  React Native (mobile)      https://github.com/AGProjects/react-native-sylkrtc.js
                             used by Sylk Mobile
  Python                     https://github.com/AGProjects/python3-sylkrtc
                             discovery, connection, video rooms, calls,
                             addressbook, the HTTPS API, with a GStreamer
                             webrtcbin media backend (optional: a client can
                             bring its own); its command line tools
                             (sylk-conference, sylk-session, sylk-addressbook,
                             sylk-account, sylk-journal) are a quick way to
                             test a gateway

A client finds the gateway of a domain from the DNS TXT record
_sylkserver.<domain>, which names a configuration document with the
WebSocket server (wsServer), the ICE servers and the conference domains.

Blink keeps messages in sync with the gateway's journal; see
https://github.com/AGProjects/blink-cocoa and
https://github.com/AGProjects/blink-qt for a SIP client using the HTTPS API.


SIP Proxy
---------

A SIP Proxy is required for one-to-one calls and messages.

A guide for setting up a SIP Proxy is available here:

https://sylkserver.com/download/#sip-proxy-setup

For the message journal and offline messaging, the SIP Proxy must fork all
SIP MESSAGEs to SylkServer.


Janus
-----

The media is done by Janus:

https://github.com/meetecho/janus-gateway

SylkServer talks to it over the Janus WebSocket API, so Janus must be built
with WebSocket support. The Debian package (Janus 1.1.2 on bookworm) is the
tested one.

The Janus settings relevant to the gateway (Janus 1.x .jcfg files in
/etc/janus; see their own comments):

1. janus.jcfg

general: {
    api_secret = "some_secret_key_shared_with_sylkserver"
}
nat: {
    ice_lite = true
    ice_tcp = true
}
plugins: {
    # keep janus.plugin.videoroom, janus.plugin.sip and janus.plugin.nosip
    disable = "libjanus_voicemail.so,libjanus_recordplay.so,libjanus_streaming.so,libjanus_echotest.so,libjanus_videocall.so,libjanus_textroom.so"
}
transports: {
    disable = "libjanus_http.so,libjanus_rabbitmq.so,libjanus_pfunix.so,libjanus_mqtt.so,libjanus_nanomsg.so"
}

2. janus.plugin.sip.jcfg (the janus backend)

general: {
    keepalive_interval = 0
    user_agent = "SylkServer (WebRTC gateway)"
    register_ttl = 600
    #local_ip = "ip_for_sip"
    #rtp_port_range = "20000-40000"
}

3. janus.plugin.videoroom.jcfg

No static rooms: comment out the rooms defined in it (an empty file is
fine). SylkServer creates the rooms as they are joined.

4. janus.transport.websockets.jcfg

general: {
    ws = true
    ws_port = 8188
    ws_interface = "lo"
    wss = false
}
admin: {
    admin_ws = false
}


Running
-------

After installing SylkServer with WebRTC support, start it and point a
browser to:

https://hostname:10888/webrtcgateway/

(the port is local_port of [WebServer] in config.ini).

Change the API secret shared by SylkServer and Janus: api_secret in
webrtcgateway.ini and in janus.jcfg.


Configuration
-------------

See webrtcgateway.ini.sample for the available options and their meaning.
